Count components, phases, and freedoms under explicit constraints (@mp-phase-rule: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @mineralogy-and-petrology, #source "../sources/Petrology-Nelson-Tulane.lingua#r_1MNEKH4WW2GQRD4FZWNRD1GDPZ", #source "../sources/MIT-Petrology-12-109.lingua#r_6Q1GZJKSZXQBM4N19CTRYG64TG", #source "../sources/Pseudossecoes-Gengo-Santos-Moraes-Szabo.lingua#r_00V3DDNE7B5SQHWP5NKFT0YJZR", #mineralogy-and-petrology "Binary-ternary-and-projected-phase-diagrams.lingua#r_57K2PVW70VR7BPDBWHVRDYSJEX") { r_250V4TCJ0KF4TXQ2SXM6VK77S8
**Read:** [Petrology — Stephen A. Nelson, Tulane EENS 2120](../sources/Petrology-Nelson-Tulane.lingua#r_1MNEKH4WW2GQRD4FZWNRD1GDPZ), all thermodynamic and phase-rule notes; [Petrology — Timothy Grove, MIT 12.109](../sources/MIT-Petrology-12-109.lingua#r_6Q1GZJKSZXQBM4N19CTRYG64TG), the complete introductory phase-diagram lectures/problems; [O uso de pseudosseções em Petrologia Metamórfica — Gengo and colleagues](../sources/Pseudossecoes-Gengo-Santos-Moraes-Szabo.lingua#r_00V3DDNE7B5SQHWP5NKFT0YJZR), the full basic phase-diagram and component-system discussion.

Components are a sufficient independent chemical basis for describing the chosen system under its constraints; they need not equal the number of reported oxides or mineral species. Reactions and imposed conditions affect independence. Explain the basis before counting degrees of freedom.

For the conventional nonreactive component representation of equilibrium, F = C−P+2 describes intensive freedoms with temperature and pressure variable under the standard assumptions. Fixing temperature or pressure changes the count appropriately. A diagram's appearance also depends on projected or externally constrained variables and whether phase composition varies.

Distinguish total phase amounts from intensive freedoms. An invariant equilibrium condition does not imply a unique bulk composition or amount of every phase. Natural texture or a metastable phase assemblage does not automatically obey an equilibrium phase-rule interpretation without a suitable domain and history account.

**Work:** derive the phase-rule reasoning for a simple pure-component and binary example, then count freedoms for a specified petrological component system. Explain what changes when pressure is fixed or a fluid chemical potential is imposed. Compare a real assemblage with alternative component/domain choices and identify evidence needed to justify each.

Read the full thermodynamics and phase-diagram treatment rather than treating the formula as a naming exercise. Later pseudosections, tie lines, and reaction boundaries will use the count to examine whether a proposed equilibrium account is internally consistent.

**Continue:** [Read phase diagrams with their basis, variables, and projection assumptions](Binary-ternary-and-projected-phase-diagrams.lingua#r_57K2PVW70VR7BPDBWHVRDYSJEX).

